and the access question you have

93
00:07:33,944 --> 00:07:34,679
identified.

94
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:41,222
Consider this example: a student who knows
the subject misses an assessment

95
00:07:41,223 --> 00:07:46,449
submission. Before assuming that they
didn’t care, what else would you want to

96
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:51,065
understand? Think about starting,
planning, holding steps in mind and

97
00:07:51,066 --> 00:07:56,359
managing time. The four moves on this
slide make those demands clearer. Publish

98
00:07:56,360 --> 00:08:01,111
assessment schedules early. Break tasks
into smaller parts with visible

99
00:08:01,112 --> 00:08:06,134
milestones. Show an exemplar of the
finished thing so students can see what

100
00:08:06,135 --> 00:08:11,700
they are working towards. Make submission
steps literal: where does the work go, in

101
00:08:11,701 --> 00:08:16,926
what format, and by when? If a student
needs help holding information in mind,

102
00:08:16,927 --> 00:08:21,746
put useful information where they can
return to it. Written first steps,

103
00:08:21,747 --> 00:08:26,905
instructions that stay visible and a clear
clock or timer are examples. Match

104
00:08:26,906 --> 00:08:31,792
them to the demand and check with students
whether they help. Think about

105
00:08:31,793 --> 00:08:36,883
homework too. How much of the task depends
on organisation and time, as well

106
00:08:36,884 --> 00:08:42,245
as subject knowledge? An unfinished task
may leave questions about access that a

107
00:08:42,246 --> 00:08:47,676
mark alone won’t answer. These supports
help students organise themselves to show

108
00:08:47,677 --> 00:08:52,970
what they know. Keep the learning purpose
clear and involve the relevant school

109
00:08:52,971 --> 00:08:58,061
staff where assessment policy or formal
adjustments sit beyond one teacher’s

110
00:08:58,062 --> 00:09:03,084
decision. Return to your brief: which
demand could you make more visible or

111
00:09:03,085 --> 00:09:03,831
manageable?

112
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:11,418
Predictability means having accessible
information about routine and change. It

113
00:09:11,419 --> 00:09:17,788
doesn’t mean every day must be identical,
or that we can promise nothing unexpected

114
00:09:17,789 --> 00:09:23,459
will happen. Think about how much
information in a secondary day is spoken

115
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:29,440
once, implied, or held by adults. It may
change between periods. If I couldn’t

116
00:09:29,441 --> 00:09:35,344
rely on remembering what someone told me,
how would I know what was happening

117
00:09:35,345 --> 00:09:40,626
across today’s timetable? A useful support
helps students answer four

118
00:09:40,627 --> 00:09:46,685
questions: what is happening, what is
changing, what comes next, and what can I

119
00:09:46,686 --> 00:09:52,977
do if the plan changes? Give information
early enough to be useful, in every class

120
00:09:52,978 --> 00:09:59,192
and on relief days. Imagine a Year Nine
student arrives at period three to find a

121
00:09:59,193 --> 00:10:04,940
relief teacher, another room and a changed
task. Could they find out before

122
00:10:04,941 --> 00:10:10,378
arriving? Is the change available
somewhere they can check, such as the

123
00:10:10,379 --> 00:10:15,971
class page or timetable app? Does the
relief plan keep the usual starting

124
00:10:15,972 --> 00:10:21,253
routine? Can the student ask for
information or support in a way that

125
00:10:21,254 --> 00:10:27,390
works for them? Return to the day audit.
When something changes, how do students

126
00:10:27,391 --> 00:10:33,682
know? Consistent routines, warning before
transitions and information in more than

127
00:10:33,683 --> 00:10:39,586
one form can help. Review the support
through actual experience and feedback.

128
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:45,388
Let’s return to six classrooms, six
teachers, one student. A support that

129
00:10:45,388 --> 00:10:50,189
works in one period may be unknown in the
next. This option is about making useful

130
00:10:50,189 --> 00:10:54,634
guidance travel. The slide describes a
short, one-page profile of strengths,

131
00:10:54,634 --> 00:10:59,258
interests, what helps and what costs. It
is written with the student and shared

132
00:10:59,258 --> 00:11:03,763
with the teachers who need it, including
relief teachers. The student remains

133
00:11:03,763 --> 00:11:08,445
involved in what is written and shared.
The example image is still a placeholder

134
00:11:08,445 --> 00:11:13,128
in this deck, so focus on the questions
rather than a finished template. Would a

135
00:11:13,128 --> 00:11:17,218
relief teacher, or the period four
teacher, know what helps before the

136
00:11:17,218 --> 00:11:21,841
lesson starts? Where would that guidance
live so it is actually seen? Who would

137
00:11:21,841 --> 00:11:26,405
update it, and how would the student
contribute? Separate useful guidance from

138
00:11:26,405 --> 00:11:31,206
unnecessary personal detail. A page that
gets lost in a shared drive won’t provide

139
00:11:31,206 --> 00:11:35,474
continuity. A clear place, a known
updating process and the student’s own

140
00:11:35,474 --> 00:11:39,919
perspective can make it more useful. Think
back to your communication notes:

141
00:11:39,919 --> 00:11:44,128
what should travel between people and
places? That question belongs in a

142
00:11:44,128 --> 00:11:46,320
handover as much as in a lesson plan.

143
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:52,934
With any visual support, start by asking
what the student needs to find,

144
00:11:52,935 --> 00:11:58,173
understand, remember or communicate. Then
choose a form that can make that

145
00:11:58,174 --> 00:12:03,484
information clear. It might be an object,
photograph, symbol, written word,

146
00:12:03,485 --> 00:12:08,723
checklist, map or choice support. In
secondary, that might be instructions

147
00:12:08,724 --> 00:12:14,249
that stay on the board or class page, a
task map, a checklist or an assessment

148
00:12:14,250 --> 00:12:19,416
calendar. Choose a format that is
respectful and suits the young person’s

149
00:12:19,417 --> 00:12:24,583
age and preferences. A support available
to the whole class may feel more

150
00:12:24,584 --> 00:12:29,104
comfortable than one that singles a
student out. Visual does not

151
00:12:29,105 --> 00:12:34,056
automatically mean accessible. The student
needs to understand what it

152
00:12:34,057 --> 00:12:39,582
represents and be able to use it at the
point of need. Keep unnecessary detail

153
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:45,251
out, explain how it works, and check its
meaning with the student. Page nineteen

154
00:12:45,252 --> 00:12:50,490
gives four quick-review questions. Can the
student understand it? Can they

155
00:12:50,491 --> 00:12:55,585
access it when they need it? Does it
reflect what is actually happening,

156
00:12:55,586 --> 00:13:00,896
including change? Does it give useful
information or a way to respond? Keep

157
00:13:00,897 --> 00:13:05,919
understanding and expressing in view. A
visual support may help someone

158
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:11,732
understand, while A A C provides ways to
communicate. Access to A A C doesn’t mean

159
00:13:11,733 --> 00:13:16,684
every spoken instruction or schedule is
understood. Keep the student’s

160
00:13:16,685 --> 00:13:22,210
communication system available in every
class, during transitions and changes,

161
00:13:22,211 --> 00:13:27,808
and recognise responses beyond speech. The
materials refer to a Visual Supports

162
00:13:27,809 --> 00:13:32,759
Checklist and an online webinar for
further learning, but their access

163
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:38,214
details still need to be confirmed. The
review questions on page nineteen are

164
00:13:38,215 --> 00:13:43,596
ready to use. A checklist supports
personalisation and consultation; it does

165
00:13:43,597 --> 00:13:48,978
not prescribe one format for every
student. Keep asking: does this give this

166
00:13:48,979 --> 00:13:53,643
student useful information they can
understand, access and act on?

167
00:13:55,161 --> 00:14:00,457
Return to your brief and the four option
headings. Which one or two options answer

168
00:14:00,458 --> 00:14:04,447
it? You’ve heard about executive
functioning meets assessment,

169
00:14:04,448 --> 00:14:09,482
predictability across the timetable,
supports that travel and visual supports.

170
00:14:09,483 --> 00:14:14,518
Sort those possibilities against the
demand in your design. Choose one or two.

171
00:14:14,519 --> 00:14:19,554
For each, write down why it fits: which
specific demand does it answer? Record

172
00:14:19,555 --> 00:14:24,917
the choice and your reason on section two
of the A three sheet. You can also follow

173
00:14:24,918 --> 00:14:29,364
the prompts on page eighteen. Give
yourselves about three minutes. If

174
00:14:29,365 --> 00:14:34,530
everything seems useful, return to the
brief and narrow the choice. We’re making

175
00:14:34,531 --> 00:14:39,370
a manageable response to a known demand.
Pause the video for your decision.

176
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:52,531
Let’s come back. Check that you have a
reason beside each option. Saying that a

177
00:14:52,532 --> 00:14:57,598
support is often helpful is different from
saying what it will make clearer or

178
00:14:57,599 --> 00:15:02,073
easier in this design. Keep that
connection visible. It will help you

179
00:15:02,074 --> 00:15:06,744
explain the design, decide what to
observe, and revise the support if it

180
00:15:06,745 --> 00:15:09,179
doesn’t help in the way you expected.

181
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:15,900
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

182
00:15:15,901 --> 00:15:21,058
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

183
00:15:21,059 --> 00:15:25,833
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

184
00:15:25,834 --> 00:15:30,863
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

185
00:15:30,864 --> 00:15:35,766
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

186
00:15:35,767 --> 00:15:40,605
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

187
00:15:40,606 --> 00:15:45,380
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

188
00:15:45,381 --> 00:15:50,156
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

189
00:15:50,157 --> 00:15:55,059
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
now examine your emerging design with

190
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:59,070
four practical questions, then test it
through use and feedback.

191
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:06,116
Use section three of your A three sheet,
with the prompts on page nineteen. Run

192
00:16:06,117 --> 00:16:11,421
four questions over your draft design. Can
students find and understand what

193
00:16:11,422 --> 00:16:16,656
they need? Can they take part in more than
one way? Can they pause, ask for

194
00:16:16,657 --> 00:16:22,031
help or recover? Is the support available
when demand increases? Consider the

195
00:16:22,032 --> 00:16:27,053
details. If help is only available by
asking aloud, is that workable for

196
00:16:27,054 --> 00:16:31,934
everyone using this routine? If
information is useful at the start but

197
00:16:31,935 --> 00:16:37,239
disappears during a transition, will it be
there when needed? Would the same

198
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:42,332
access to participation, help, pause and
recovery remain available with a

199
00:16:42,333 --> 00:16:47,778
different teacher or on a relief day?
These are questions to test against your

200
00:16:47,779 --> 00:16:53,013
setting, rather than assumptions about a
student. Tick each question as you

201
00:16:53,014 --> 00:16:58,530
consider it. Where the answer is ‘not
yet’, adjust the design before moving on.

202
00:16:58,531 --> 00:17:03,410
Allow about six minutes. Pause the video
while your trio completes the

203
00:17:03,411 --> 00:17:04,825
accessibility check.

204
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:17,632
Come back to the changes you made. The
check should influence the design, not

205
00:17:17,633 --> 00:17:21,669
just record that you discussed
accessibility. Keep any unresolved

206
00:17:21,670 --> 00:17:26,462
question beside the plan. This is an
initial check. You’ll still need to find

207
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:31,381
out what happens when students use the
support and have a way to give feedback.

208
00:17:31,382 --> 00:17:35,859
Access is demonstrated through experience,
rather than guaranteed by the

209
00:17:35,860 --> 00:17:37,499
resource we have prepared.

